Move the uptime-clock time stamp into gpui_apple, for iOS and macOS touches.

What: `gpui_apple::process_info::uptime_instant(timestamp: f64) -> Option<Instant>`: an event time on the uptime
clock (`UITouch.timestamp`, `NSEvent.timestamp`, both seconds since boot as `NSProcessInfo.systemUptime` counts)
moved onto GPUI's clock by its age; a stamp later than now counts as now. It is the maths patch 0020 put in
gpui_ios's `touch_time_stamp`, which goes: gpui_ios's touch now calls `uptime_instant(touch.timestamp())`, and
macOS's direct touches (patch 0026) call it with `NSEvent.timestamp`. Behaviour on iOS is unchanged.

Ported from flutter/flutter engine/src/flutter/shell/platform/darwin/ios/framework/Source/FlutterViewController.mm
(`dispatchTouches:`, `pointer_data.time_stamp = touch.timestamp * kMicrosecondsPerSecond`) and
engine/src/flutter/shell/platform/darwin/macos/framework/Source/FlutterViewController.mm (its pointer events'
`.timestamp = static_cast<size_t>(event.timestamp * USEC_PER_SEC)`) @ 3.50.1
(ae9b586f34a8443573f82ef57c3bf914a908cbf7). Copyright 2014 The Flutter Authors. BSD-3-Clause, see
third_party/flutter/LICENSE. The port header sits on the function.

Why: gpui_ios and gpui_macos both depend on gpui_apple, the crate that already wraps `NSProcessInfo`; one copy of
the clock maths serves both, instead of macOS copying iOS's. No cfg beyond the crate's own (Apple targets).

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

